PROMPT_CASE_RUN review status=Completed error=None result=Some("**Review of `src/limit.rs`** (verified directly against the file — 4 lines total, no callers, tests, or crate root exist in this workspace).\n\n### Finding 1 — Inclusive range + unguarded indexing panics on all inputs of length ≤ 3, and returns 4 items instead of 3 (high)\n\n**Location:** `src/limit.rs:3`\n\n```rust\n(0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n```\n\nTwo independent boundary errors in one expression:\n\n1. **Off-by-one:** `0..=MAX_LINES` with `MAX_LINES = 3` (`src/limit.rs:1`) is an *inclusive* range — it iterates `i == 0,1,2,3`, i.e. 4 items. For a function named `first_lines` capped at 3 lines, an exactly-full input returns 4 items, silently exceeding the limit.\n2. **Guaranteed panic:** `lines[i]` is an unguarded slice index, and the highest index visited is always 3. Every input with fewer than 4 elements panics: `first_lines(&[])`, `first_lines(&[\"a\"])`, `first_lines(&[\"a\",\"b\"])`, and `first_lines(&[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"])` all abort with `index out of bounds: the len is N but the index is 3`. Only inputs of length ≥ 4 return at all. The panic fires inside the iterator chain, so short input kills the whole call rather than being handled as the normal short-list case — which is exactly what a `first_lines` helper exists to accept.\n\n**Smallest fix:** clamp via iteration instead of indexing:\n```rust\nlines.iter().take(MAX_LINES).map(|s| s.to_string()).collect()\n```\nThis returns `[]` for empty input, `len` items for `len <= 3`, and exactly 3 for longer input — no panic on any input.\n\n### What was checked and found clean\n\n- No other logic, arithmetic, state, or `unsafe` exists in the file, so overflow, state-corruption, and concurrency categories are vacuous.\n- A workspace-wide search found no references to `first_lines` or `MAX_LINES` outside their definitions, so no caller semantics conflict with the fix.\n- Not verified: nothing was executed (sandbox command execution is unavailable in this session); the panic follows deterministically from Rust indexing semantics over `0..=3` on a slice.\n\n**No files were changed.**")
