llers, tests, or sibling modules exist in this workspace to constrain the intended semantics (no `main.rs`/`lib.rs`/`Cargo.toml`/`tests/`), so the contract is inferred from the names only.\n\n### Finding 1 — Out-of-bounds index panics for every input with ≤ 3 elements (high)\n\n1. **Path/lines:** `src/limit.rs:1-3`, specifically line 3.\n2. **Code:**\n```rust\npub const MAX_LINES: usize = 3;\npub fn first_lines(lines: &[&str]) -> Vec<String> {\n    (0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n}\n```\n3. **Severity:** high\n4. **Why it's wrong:** two independent boundary errors in one expression:\n   - **Off-by-one:** `0..=MAX_LINES` is inclusive, producing 4 iterations (`i == 0,1,2,3`). For a function named `first_lines` with a `MAX_LINES = 3` cap, the intent is clearly at most 3 lines. With an exactly-full input of 3 elements it returns **4** items, silently exceeding the documented limit.\n   - **Panic:** `lines[i]` is an unguarded index. Any slice with `len() <= 3` panics with `index out of bounds: the len is N but the index is N`, because the highest index visited is always 3. Concrete triggers: `first_lines(&[])`, `first_lines(&[\"a\"])`, `first_lines(&[\"a\",\"b\"])`, `first_lines(&[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"])`. All four panic; only `len >= 4` returns.\n   - There is no length check, no `get()`, no error handling, and the panic occurs inside `.map(...).collect()` rather than at the call site, so the failure surfaces as an aborted request/crash rather than a handled short-input case. This is the classic \"truncated input kills the whole call\" pattern for a function whose whole purpose is to accept short input.\n   - This is unreachable-by-tests today: the crate root doesn't exist, so no test exercises it. Any first real caller passing a user-supplied or file-derived list will hit it on short input.\n5. **Smallest fix:** clamp the range instead of indexing blindly:\n```rust\npub fn first_lines(lines: &[&str]) -> Vec<String> {\n    lines.iter().take(MAX_LINES).map(|s| s.to_string()).collect()\n}\n```\n   This returns `[]` for empty input, `len` items for `len <= 3`, and exactly `MAX_LINES` for longer input — no panic on any input. (If the historical inclusive semantics were truly intended, it would need `lines.iter().take(MAX_LINES + 1)` plus a comment, but nothing in the file supports that reading.)\n\n## Coverage limits — what I read and what I did not\n\n**Read (complete, this is the entire project):**\n- `src/limit.rs` — all 4 lines.\n- Workspace root listing, `src/` listing, and a full recursive glob: the only other files are `.aster/` metadata and `store/v1/...` agent session-store JSON/lock/WAL files, which are unrelated runtime data, not source or tests. I did not treat them as project code.\n\n**Searched, zero hits:**\n- `first_lines` / `MAX_LINES` referenced anywhere other than their definitions (no callers anywhere in the workspace).\n- `mod lim\n\n[398 bytes omitted from delegated task output]\n\ncannot run, so **no executable verification was performed**. The panic behavior follows directly from Rust indexing semantics over `0..=3` on `&[&str]`, which is deterministic and needs no tooling to establish.\n- No integer overflow/underflow concern applies: `usize` literals `0..=3` cannot overflow, and there is no arithmetic.\n- No concurrency/aliasing concern applies: the function is a pure `&[&str] -> Vec<String>` transformation with no shared state, no interior mutability, and no `unsafe`.\n\n**Bottom line:** exactly one actionable defect (high): the inclusive range plus unguarded indexing makes `first_lines` panic on all inputs of length ≤ 3 and return an over-limit count of 4 when it does succeed. No other defects found; all other categories in scope are vacuous for this 4-line file.", exit_code: 0, metadata: Some(Object {"task_id": String("task-a5388876-adaa-45d5-b026-c45b30a2dae3"), "session_id": String("task-run-task-a5388876-adaa-45d5-b026-c45b30a2dae3"), "agent": String("review"), "success": Bool(true)